pin-both-is-cheaper-than-choosing

HIS ASK, 2026-09-17: "I would like to work a little with the open questions to see if we can move
toward 'pin both where possible' vs planning to have the session we kick off with your suggested
prompt make safer more defensible choice."

THE ANSWER IS A SHAPE, AND IT WAS BUILT BEFORE IT WAS PROPOSED.  bin/sw1ng carries it, live, in
nine lines, and tests/sw1ng-tests.sh has three named tests that fail if it is removed.

THE SHAPE.  When a rule admits two honest readings and only the keeper can settle which:

  1. NAME both readings, each with a short label a person can say aloud.  Not "option A" -- STRICT
     and PER-CAUSE.  A reading without a name gets rounded off to the other one within a week.
  2. COMPUTE both, every time, from the same state.  Two functions, not one function and a comment.
  3. AGREE OUT LOUD where they agree, which is most of the time.  This is the part that makes it
     cheap: a pinned fork is silent until it bites, so pinning costs nothing on the common path.
  4. REFUSE where they diverge -- a distinct exit code, both readings printed with what each would
     have you do, and the fork stated as a question the keeper can answer in one sentence.
  5. Say what STOPS the refusal: "rule it and this branch stops appearing."  A pin that cannot be
     un-pinned is a bug wearing a doctrine's coat.

WHY THIS BEATS "MAKE THE SAFER, MORE DEFENSIBLE CHOICE".  A safer choice is made once, quietly, by
whoever was in the chair, and it is indistinguishable afterwards from a ruling.  The estate then
carries a rule nobody made -- which is the exact failure ING-0001's cost line names: "the answer
quietly hardens by accretion instead of by ruling."  A pin cannot harden.  It sits there costing
nothing until the day the two readings actually differ, and on that day it hands the keeper a
one-sentence question instead of a dig.

THE MEASUREMENT, such as it is.  On a task that has spent its redesign and three further swings,
STRICT says the counter is exhausted and PER-CAUSE says another cycle is reachable.  That is the
only state in the whole loop where they differ.  So the fork costs: nine lines of program, three
tests, and one refusal that fires in one state out of the dozen the loop can be in.

WHERE PINNING BOTH IS NOT AVAILABLE, and this is the limit rather than a caveat:

  - When the two readings cannot both be COMPUTED from the state on hand.  A reading that needs a
    fact nobody has is not a reading, it is a wish.  Fall back to a reversible default, NAMED in
    one line, with the observation that would send you back.
  - When holding both would double a stored artefact.  Two live schemas is the ledgers fork, and
    its own room found a strictly dominant first move rather than an even fork; pinning there would
    have been padding to two.
  - When one reading is refuted rather than merely unchosen.  A refuted reading is parked with its
    wormhole, not pinned.  eclipse-pass owns that verb.

THE FALSIFIER.  This is wrong if a pinned fork ever costs something on the common path.  Check:
`sh bin/sw1ng --prove-red` -- the refusal fires in exactly one of thirteen checks, and the other
twelve never mention the two readings.  A pin that shows up in ordinary output is not a pin, it is
a nag, and it should be deleted.

SECOND FALSIFIER.  It is also wrong if a pin survives its ruling.  Check: when he rules the counter,
one of cap_A/cap_B is deleted, the exit-9 branch goes, and three tests go with it.  If that removal
is not trivial, the pin was welded rather than pinned.
